---
---

<div class="mt-8">
  <!-- Main content container with simple border -->
  <div class="bg-base-100 border-2 border-primary/30 rounded-lg p-5 hover:border-primary/50 transition-colors duration-300">
    <!-- Title -->
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold flex items-center mb-4">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 mr-2 text-primary" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
      </svg>
      <span class="font-mono tracking-tight text-primary">Security Analysis: How This Message System Works</span>
    </h3>
  
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm">
      <div>
        <p class="border-l-2 border-success pl-3 py-1.5 hover:bg-success/5 transition-colors">
          <span class="font-semibold text-success font-mono">[✓]</span> 
          <span class="font-medium text-success">Static Website Protection:</span> 
          <span class="text-base-content/80">This website is served by GitHub Pages over HTTPS. The website files themselves are protected in transit, but GitHub Pages only serves static content.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      
      <div>
        <p class="border-l-2 border-warning pl-3 py-1.5 hover:bg-warning/5 transition-colors">
          <span class="font-semibold text-warning font-mono">[!]</span> 
          <span class="font-medium text-warning">Message Transmission:</span>
          <span class="text-base-content/80">Your messages are sent directly from your browser to a Cloudflare Worker over HTTPS (encrypted in transit) but are readable in plaintext inside that Worker. A plain message is delivered together with your IP address, approximate location, network provider and browser details. <code class="text-primary">/encrypt</code> seals the message body and your browser details, so the Worker and Telegram see only ciphertext for those. Your IP and approximate location are still attached in the clear, because the Worker must see your IP to route the request and cannot encrypt it to my key.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      
      <div>
        <p class="border-l-2 border-error pl-3 py-1.5 hover:bg-error/5 transition-colors">
          <span class="font-semibold text-error font-mono">[×]</span> 
          <span class="font-medium text-error">Third-Party Processing:</span>
          <span class="text-base-content/80">The Worker then forwards the message to Telegram through their API. Both Cloudflare and Telegram can technically access the message content.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  
    <!-- Simple divider -->
    <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-base-300">
      <p class="text-sm text-base-content/80 mb-3">
        This system shows why <strong class="text-primary">end-to-end encryption</strong> matters: your browser connects to this website over HTTPS, but when you send a message, intermediaries (the Cloudflare Worker, Telegram) can still read the content. 
        The solution? <strong class="text-primary">Encrypt messages in your browser before sending them.</strong> Try it yourself with <code class="bg-base-200 px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-primary text-xs font-mono border border-primary/20">/encrypt &lt;your message&gt;</code> in the terminal above.
      </p>
      
      <!-- Research focus -->
      <div class="mt-3 p-3 bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded hover:border-primary/40 transition-colors">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-base-content/90 leading-relaxed">
          <span class="text-primary font-mono text-xs mr-1">//</span>
          My research focuses on <span class="text-primary font-semibold">building the mathematical foundations</span> that make secure encryption possible: 
          designing and analyzing <span class="text-primary font-medium">block ciphers</span>, <span class="text-primary font-medium">hash functions</span>, and <span class="text-primary font-medium">cryptographic primitives</span>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
